SwiftNIO on Windows: Reqly's fixes to its WSAPoll event loop and to reading, by the Reqly
authors, under SwiftNIO's license (Apache License 2.0).

1. A socket that a channel deregistered stayed in WSAPoll's set until the next poll returned, and
   Windows gives a closed socket's handle to the next socket opened. A new connection could then
   be reported on the old entry, or its own entry lost: the event loop hung at full CPU, or died
   with WSAENOTSOCK. Deregistered entries now leave the set at once, and are removed before the
   next poll. Upstream aims at the same with apple/swift-nio#3706.
2. WSAPoll reports POLLHUP when the peer only stops sending, while it may still read, and NIO
   took it for a reset, so half-closed connections broke. POLLHUP alone is now the end of input,
   as EPOLLRDHUP is on Linux, and a socket that hung up is left out of polling while its channel
   asks for nothing WSAPoll can tell it, since WSAPoll reports POLLHUP on every poll after.
   Upstream: apple/swift-nio#3715.
3. Windows.recv, in Windows.swift, failed with WSAEWOULDBLOCK when a nonblocking socket had
   nothing to read, which is EAGAIN on POSIX, and the channel closed. It now reports that it would
   block. 0001 makes the same change to the wrappers in BSDSocketAPIWindows.swift, as do
   apple/swift-nio#3705, #3708 and #3716; none of them changes this one.

